b/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537815 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8bc18207-a6fd-5437-a68d-b3d6fef6d67e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537838 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a8f6c78e-2502-540d-9f7b-11049bb63129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537933 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537933 #edb/ref 1069 "q-226103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537933 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537933 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/321c16127ae1999e4ec42024f35cd076930618588fe378b9ca16a4ee4eabd58a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-0.5x\\,i-0.5y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537933 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7933960a-e5cd-5862-9088-b9c1f61b494c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537933 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/abe90a592e098a4656577f3a2303669dccc126baff738397836c8f96c26b5389.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-0.5x\\,i-0.5y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-0.5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-0.5{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-16)=-64.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537939 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537939 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537939 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537939 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/32d4722f4a1dd1cfae3fb22cde7399dcb66623e484bcd5989fd2de246a905b51.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-3\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537939 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90bd3eab-412f-52dc-83f5-2f8877931db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537939 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-3\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
